---
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import RelatedPages from '../components/RelatedPages.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { lastUpdatedIso } from '../config/pages';
import { absoluteUrl } from '../config/site';
import { supportingJsonLd } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'How Fast Does the World Actually Send Morse Code?';
const description =
  'We analysed 16.7 million Morse transmissions from the Reverse Beacon Network. The typical station sends at 24 WPM in every season. Data, charts and method.';
const pageUrl = absoluteUrl('/morse-code-speed-statistics/');
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: absoluteUrl('/') },
  { name: 'Morse Code Speed Statistics', url: pageUrl },
];
const jsonLd = supportingJsonLd({
  name: 'How Fast Does the World Actually Send Morse Code?',
  description,
  url: pageUrl,
  crumbs,
  dateModified: lastUpdatedIso('/morse-code-speed-statistics/'),
});

const link = 'text-accent underline underline-offset-4';
const h2 = 'text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight';
const tableClass = 'w-full border-collapse text-left text-base';
const thClass = 'border-b border-line py-2 pr-3 font-medium text-muted';
const tdClass = 'border-b border-line py-2 pr-3';
const speedRows = [
  { lab: 'Under 15 WPM', width: '14.8%', val: '4.3%' },
  { lab: '15–19 WPM', width: '49.3%', val: '14.3%' },
  { lab: '20–24 WPM', width: '100%', val: '29.0%' },
  { lab: '25–29 WPM', width: '97.2%', val: '28.2%' },
  { lab: '30–34 WPM', width: '60.3%', val: '17.5%' },
  { lab: '35–39 WPM', width: '19.7%', val: '5.7%' },
  { lab: '40+ WPM', width: '2.8%', val: '0.8%' },
] as const;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd} suffix={false} ogType="article">
  <article class="grid max-w-3xl gap-8 text-[1.0625rem] leading-7">
    <Breadcrumb items={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { label: 'Morse Code Speed Statistics' }]} />
    <header class="grid gap-3">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How Fast Does the World Actually Send Morse Code?</h1>
      <p class="text-muted">
        We measured real Morse transmissions recorded by the Reverse Beacon Network, across five weeks in four seasons.
        The answer is slower, and steadier, than most people guess.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-6 py-8 text-center">
      <p class="text-6xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-accent sm:text-7xl">
        24 <span class="text-2xl text-ink sm:text-3xl">WPM</span>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
        The median sending speed of a typical station on air.<br />
        Half of all stations send between 19.5 and 28 WPM.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-4 py-3">
        <p class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent">16,703,487</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">Morse transmissions (spots) analysed</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-4 py-3">
        <p class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent">110,003</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">Different stations measured</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-4 py-3">
        <p class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent">5 weeks</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">Spread across winter, spring, summer and autumn</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-4 py-3">
        <p class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent">23–24 WPM</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">The median station's speed in every single week</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="cv grid gap-3">
      <h2 class={h2}>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        The typical station on the bands sends Morse code at about <strong>24 words per minute</strong>. That figure is
        not an average pulled up by a few very fast operators: it is the median, the station exactly in the middle of
        the pack. And it barely moves. We sampled one ordinary week in each season of the year, deliberately avoiding
        the biggest contests, and the median station sent at 23 or 24 WPM every time. The seasons change the bands, the
        propagation and the number of people on air. They do not change how fast the typical operator sends.
      </p>
      <p>
        Counting every transmission instead of every station gives a slightly higher figure, a median of 25 WPM, because
        fast, active stations get heard more often. We report the per-station figure as the headline, because it
        describes the typical operator rather than the typical transmission.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cv grid gap-3">
      <h2 class={h2}>Key findings</h2>
      <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>The middle is comfortable:</strong> half of all stations send between 19.5 and 28 WPM. Only 1 station
          in 10 sends at 16 WPM or slower, and only 1 in 10 at 32 WPM or faster.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Very fast sending is rare:</strong> fewer than 1 in 100 transmissions (0.8%) are sent at 40 WPM or
          faster.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Most Morse lives at 20–29 WPM:</strong> 57.3% of all transmissions fall in that range.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Two bands carry three fifths of all CW:</strong> 20 metres (5,388,114 spots, median 25 WPM) and 40
          metres (4,674,363 spots, median 25 WPM).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The fastest big bands</strong> are 15 and 80 metres (median 27 WPM); the slowest is 10 metres (median
          20 WPM).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Europe sends a touch faster than North America</strong> in this data: a median of 25 WPM against 23
          WPM, on 64.4% and 26.0% of spots respectively. Treat that gap with care; it also reflects where the network's
          receivers are.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The result survives its worst week:</strong> dropping the noisiest sampled week entirely, a December
          week containing a 160-metre contest, the median station still sends at exactly 24.0 WPM.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="cv grid gap-3">
      <h2 class={h2}>Where the transmissions fall</h2>
      <p>Share of all 16.7 million transmissions, by sending speed:</p>
      <div class="speed-chart grid gap-2">
        {
          speedRows.map((row) => (
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <div class="w-[6.75rem] shrink-0 text-right text-sm text-muted">{row.lab}</div>
              <div class="speed-track">
                <div class="speed-bar" style={`width:${row.width}`}></div>
              </div>
              <div class="w-12 shrink-0 font-semibold">{row.val}</div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        Read it as a hill centred on the low twenties. The long, thin tail to the right is the contest crowd; the bulk
        of everyday Morse sits well below it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cv grid gap-3">
      <h2 class={h2}>Speed by band</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class={tableClass}>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th class={thClass} scope="col">Band</th>
              <th class={`${thClass} text-right`} scope="col">Transmissions</th>
              <th class={`${thClass} text-right`} scope="col">Share</th>
              <th class={`${thClass} text-right`} scope="col">Median WPM</th>
              <th class={`${thClass} text-right`} scope="col">Mean WPM</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>20m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>5,388,114</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>32.3%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25.3</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>40m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>4,674,363</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>28.0%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25.2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>15m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>1,524,375</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>9.1%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>27</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>26.5</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>80m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>1,490,037</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>8.9%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>27</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>26.3</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>30m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>1,074,253</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>6.4%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>22</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>22.2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>10m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>819,654</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>4.9%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>20</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>20.8</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>160m *</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>775,068</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>4.6%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>27</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>26.8</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>17m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>683,767</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>4.1%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>23</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>23.2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>12m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>187,820</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>1.1%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>23</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>23.8</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>60m</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>46,831</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>0.3%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>22</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>22.7</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        * The 160m row is the one figure in this study to quote with care: 79% of its spots come from a single contest
        weekend in the December sample week (the ARRL 160-Meter Contest). In the other four weeks, the band's median is
        23 to 26 WPM on far fewer transmissions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cv grid gap-3">
      <h2 class={h2}>Speed by continent</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class={tableClass}>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th class={thClass} scope="col">Continent of the station heard</th>
              <th class={`${thClass} text-right`} scope="col">Transmissions</th>
              <th class={`${thClass} text-right`} scope="col">Share</th>
              <th class={`${thClass} text-right`} scope="col">Median WPM</th>
              <th class={`${thClass} text-right`} scope="col">Mean WPM</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>Europe</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>10,764,507</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>64.4%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25.4</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>North America</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>4,346,665</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>26.0%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>23</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>23.7</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>Asia</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>981,337</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>5.9%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25.5</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>Africa</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>274,008</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>1.6%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>27</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>26.3</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>South America</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>207,632</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>1.2%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>25.2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class={tdClass}>Oceania</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>129,338</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>0.8%</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>24</td>
              <td class={`${tdClass} text-right`}>24.0</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        These shares map the Reverse Beacon Network's receiver coverage as much as world activity: most skimmer
        receivers sit in Europe and North America, so stations there are heard far more often.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cv grid gap-3">
      <h2 class={h2}>What this means if you are learning Morse code</h2>
      <p>
        Learners often imagine the bands are full of operators sending far faster than they could ever copy. The data
        says otherwise.
      </p>
      <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Aim for the middle, not the tail.</strong> The typical station sends at about 24 WPM, and half of all
          stations sit between 19.5 and 28 WPM. If you can copy comfortably at around 20 WPM, you are already within
          reach of the middle of the distribution.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nobody expects 40 WPM.</strong> Fewer than 1 in 100 transmissions go that fast. Contest-speed sending
          is a speciality, not the entry ticket.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Slow company exists.</strong> One station in ten sends at 16 WPM or slower, so even early on, there
          are transmissions on air at learner-friendly speeds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Speed is a habit, not a season.</strong> The median held at 23 to 24 WPM in winter, spring, summer and
          autumn alike. Whenever you get on air, the pace you practised for is the pace you will find.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Practise at realistic speeds with our free <a class={link} href="/">Morse code translator</a>: learn the shapes
        from the <a class={link} href="/morse-code-alphabet/">Morse code alphabet chart</a>, then test your ear by
        decoding real audio with the{' '}
        <a class={link} href="/morse-code-audio-translator/">Morse code audio translator</a>. Set the player near 20 WPM
        and you are training for the bands as they actually are.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cv grid gap-3">
      <h2 class={h2}>How we measured this (methodology)</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Data.</strong> Every figure comes from the Reverse Beacon Network's public archive of spots: automated
        receiving stations (skimmers) around the world that listen for callsigns, decode them, and report the frequency,
        band and estimated sending speed. We downloaded the daily archive files for five complete Monday-to-Sunday
        weeks: 1–7 December 2025, 2–8 March 2026, 11–17 May 2026, 17–23 August 2026, and 26 October – 1 November 2026.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Week choice.</strong> Weeks were screened to avoid the six biggest CW contests (CQ WW CW, ARRL DX CW, CQ
        WPX CW, IARU HF, WAE CW and ARRL Sweepstakes CW), whose weekends would have swamped ordinary activity. Three
        sampled weekends still held smaller CW contests; the largest footprints are disclosed wherever they touch a
        figure, and removing the single noisiest week leaves the headline unchanged at 24.0 WPM.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Cleaning.</strong> Of 16,844,912 archived rows, we kept CW transmissions with a reported speed between 5
        and 80 WPM, leaving 16,703,487 spots (0.03% were dropped as out-of-range decoder artefacts). Each station was
        identified by callsign; portable suffixes count as separate callsigns, so the true number of individual
        operators is slightly below 110,003.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Honest limits.</strong> Speeds are machine estimates made by the skimmer software, in whole WPM, and can
        be off by roughly 1 to 2 WPM on any single transmission, especially hand-sent code; medians across millions of
        transmissions are far more stable than any individual reading. The network hears stations that call CQ within
        range of a receiver, so quiet one-to-one chats and regions with few receivers are under-counted, and continent
        shares reflect receiver coverage as much as activity. Five weeks in one year is a strong sample of ordinary
        weeks, not a census.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cv grid gap-3">
      <h2 class={h2}>Cite this study</h2>
      <p>Cite this study: Mir Labs / translatorcodemorse.com</p>
      <p class="rounded-xl border border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-4 py-3 text-sm">
        Mir Labs, “How fast does the world actually send Morse code?”, translatorcodemorse.com, November 2026. Based on
        16,703,487 Reverse Beacon Network spots across 110,003 stations in five sampled weeks between December 2025 and
        November 2026.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        Data: the <a class={link} href="https://www.reversebeacon.net/">Reverse Beacon Network</a> and its volunteer
        skimmer operators, whose receivers recorded every transmission in this study. The network asks that results be
        shared back with the community; this page is that sharing, with thanks.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        Study by Mir Labs, makers of the free <a class={link} href="/">Morse code translator</a> at
        translatorcodemorse.com.
      </p>
    </section>

    <RelatedPages
      links={[
        { href: '/', label: 'Translate text to Morse code' },
        { href: '/morse-code-alphabet/', label: 'Morse code alphabet chart' },
        { href: '/morse-code-audio-translator/', label: 'Decode Morse audio to text' },
      ]}
    />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .speed-track {
    flex: 1;
    height: 1.625rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
    background: var(--logo-chip);
  }
  .speed-bar {
    height: 1.625rem;
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
    background: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
